  "response": "When stylometric indicators and archival documents point toward different authors, scholars do not simply tally the two bodies of evidence against each other; they ask which has been properly controlled, and they test the weaker against the stronger. The contested case of the Funeral Elegy illustrates the procedure in detail. That Thomas Thorpe entered the elegy in the Stationers' Register in February 1612 shows that the poem \"A Funerall Elegye in memory of the late Vertuous Maister William Peter\" was published in 1612. The Stationers' Register entry for the elegy names William Shakespeare as its author, and it was precisely because the documentary record carried that name that Donald Foster argued in a 1989 study that William Shakespeare wrote the elegy.\n\nSubsequently Foster compiled a computerized database of Shakespeare's vocabulary called SHAXICON, designed to track the poet's lexical habits across his working career. Foster released SHAXICON as open-source software in 1996. Whereas the compilation of that database was an immense scholarly undertaking in its own right, the database was never made publicly available for independent verification; that permanent unavailability cannot be squared with an open-source release in 1996. The episode shows why adjudication turns on the auditability of quantitative evidence quite as much as on the results it yields.\n\nThe methodological demands of stylometry explain this asymmetry between the two kinds of proof. Stylometric methods commonly measure the relative frequencies of common function words, a practice formalized in the mid-twentieth century. Differences of genre and date of composition can distort stylometric signals. A separate requirement concerns the comparative baseline: reliable attribution requires a control corpus of texts by rival candidate authors from the same period and genre. Without such a benchmark, a measured resemblance to one candidate remains uninterpretable, since the features in question may be no more characteristic of Shakespeare than of a dozen contemporaries writing in the same mode.\n\nThat requirement for a properly constituted control corpus led Foster to concede in 2002 that Shakespeare was not the author of the elegy. In other words, Foster publicly abandoned his claim of Shakespearean authorship of the elegy in 2002. His withdrawal confirms that William Shakespeare did not write the Funeral Elegy. At least one of that conclusion and the further proposition that John Ford did not write the Funeral Elegy must hold, and perhaps both do. Adjudication of such disputes therefore proceeds by subjecting each line of argument to the other's discipline: title pages and register entries are examined for what they can and cannot certify, while computational results are credited only when the underlying data and the comparative controls are open to re-examination by other hands.",
